@rem Exercises for lesson 02_pointers (C++ track). Format: docs/CONTENT_FORMAT.md

@exercise cpp-ptr-theory-address
@type theory
@lesson 02_pointers
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which operator gives the address of a variable? Write the symbol.
@answer
& | address-of | the address-of operator | ampersand
@hint
It is the same symbol that marks a reference parameter.
@explain
&x is the address of x, and its type is a pointer to the type of x (&i is int* when i is an int).

@exercise cpp-ptr-choice-deref
@type choice
@lesson 02_pointers
@difficulty 1
@prompt
After  int i = 5; int* p = &i; *p = 10;  what is the value of i?
@choices
- 5
* 10
- The address of i
- It does not compile
@explain
*p is the variable p points to, which is i. Assigning to *p therefore changes i.

@exercise cpp-ptr-predict-update
@type predict
@lesson 02_pointers
@difficulty 1
@prompt
What does this code print?
@code
int a = 3;
int* p = &a;
*p = *p + 2;
cout << a << endl;
@answer
5
@explain
*p is a, so a becomes 3 + 2 = 5.

@exercise cpp-ptr-fill-sum
@type fill
@lesson 02_pointers
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Fill in the blank so that sum returns the sum of numValues integers, using pointer arithmetic.
@code
int sum(int* ptr, int numValues) {
    int total = 0;
    for (int k = 0; k < numValues; k++) {
        total += ___;
    }
    return total;
}
@answer
*(ptr + k) | *(ptr+k) | ptr[k]
@match loose
@hint
ptr + k is the address of element k; follow it with *.
@explain
a[i] is the same as *(a + i): ptr + k moves k elements forward and * reads that element.

@exercise cpp-ptr-bug-offbyone
@type bug
@lesson 02_pointers
@difficulty 2
@prompt
This code should print the five numbers of the array. Click the buggy line, then type the corrected line.
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int arr[] = {4, 6, 7, 8, 9};
    int* ptr = arr;
    for (int k = 0; k <= 5; k++) {
        cout << *(ptr + k) << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;
    return 0;
}
@bugline 7
@answer
for (int k = 0; k < 5; k++) {
@match loose
@explain
The valid indexes are 0 to 4. With k <= 5 the loop also reads arr[5], one element past the end (undefined behavior).

@exercise cpp-ptr-theory-step
@type theory
@lesson 02_pointers
@difficulty 2
@prompt
On a typical system, by how many bytes does p + 1 move an int pointer p? Write the number.
@answer
4 | 4 bytes | sizeof(int)
@explain
ptr + 1 moves n * sizeof(type) bytes: 4 bytes for a 4-byte int.

@exercise cpp-ptr-theory-nullptr
@type theory
@lesson 02_pointers
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which keyword means "this pointer points to nothing" in C++?
@answer
nullptr
@explain
Set pointers to nullptr when they have no target and test them before dereferencing.

@exercise cpp-ptr-choice-null
@type choice
@lesson 02_pointers
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Which of these can be null: a pointer parameter or a reference parameter?
@choices
* Only the pointer
- Only the reference
- Both
- Neither
@explain
A pointer may hold nullptr; a reference must always refer to an existing object.

@exercise cpp-ptr-predict-trace
@type predict
@lesson 02_pointers
@difficulty 3
@prompt
Trace this code line by line (it is the exercise from the pointer-to-pointer section). What does the last line print?
@code
int i = 2, j = 8;
int* ptr;
int** pptr;
ptr = &i;
pptr = &ptr;
*ptr = 7;
**pptr = 12;
ptr = &j;
**pptr = 34;
*pptr = &i;
*ptr = -25;
cout << i << " " << j << endl;
@answer
-25 34
@hint
Write down which variable ptr points to after every line.
@explain
i=7, then i=12 (through pptr), ptr now points to j, j=34, *pptr = &i makes ptr point to i again, and *ptr = -25 sets i. Final state: i = -25, j = 34.

@exercise cpp-ptr-predict-array
@type predict
@lesson 02_pointers
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this code print?
@code
int a[4] = {10, 20, 30, 40};
int* p = a + 1;
p++;
*p = *p + 5;
cout << a[1] << " " << a[2] << " " << *(a + 2) << endl;
@answer
20 35 35
@explain
p starts at a[1] and p++ moves it to a[2]. That element becomes 35. a[2] and *(a + 2) are the same element.

@exercise cpp-ptr-fill-until-zero
@type fill
@lesson 02_pointers
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Add the elements of {4, 6, 7, 8, 9, 0} until a 0 is reached. Fill in the for loop: for (int *p = a; ....; ....)
@code
int a[] = {4, 6, 7, 8, 9, 0};
int total = 0;
for (int *p = a; ___; ___) {
    total += *p;
}
@answer
*p != 0 | *p
p++ | ++p | p = p + 1 | p += 1
@explain
The loop continues while *p is not 0 and moves to the next element with p++.

@exercise cpp-ptr-fill-print
@type fill
@lesson 02_pointers
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Print the 5 integers of arr using pointer arithmetic.
@code
int arr[] = {4, 6, 7, 8, 9};
int* ptr = arr;
int len = 5;
for (int k = 0; k < len; k++) {
    cout << ___ << " ";
}
@answer
*(ptr + k) | ptr[k]
@explain
*(ptr + k) is the element k places after the one ptr points to.

@exercise cpp-ptr-fill-swap
@type fill
@lesson 02_pointers
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Complete the swap function that takes two pointers.
@code
void swapPtr(int* x, int* y) {
    int z = ___;
    *x = *y;
    *y = ___;
}
@answer
*x
z
@explain
Save the value x points to in z, copy *y into *x, then store the saved value in *y.

@exercise cpp-ptr-bug-address
@type bug
@lesson 02_pointers
@difficulty 1
@prompt
The program should print 5 through a pointer. Click the buggy line, then type the corrected line.
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int i = 5;
    int* ptr = i;
    cout << *ptr << endl;
    return 0;
}
@bugline 6
@answer
int* ptr = &i;
@explain
A pointer must hold an address: use &i.
